2026-08-17 fp8_einsum SM12x Triton fallback + dynamo boundary:
- Repeated runtime failure in deepseek_v4 fp8 o_proj einsum on SM12x (GB10): DeepGEMM fp8_einsum
  dies on layout.hpp:107 scale assert, then (after the Triton fallback) torch.compile traces into
  the dispatcher and chokes on get_device_capability() (lru_cache) / get_tensor_model_parallel_rank().
- Fix (port from jasl codex/ds4-sm120-min-enable):
  - Added vllm/models/deepseek_v4/nvidia/ops/fp8_einsum.py (Triton SM12x einsum kernels +
    deepseek_v4_fp8_einsum dispatcher + config).
  - o_proj.py now calls deepseek_v4_fp8_einsum; compute_fp8_einsum_recipe delegates to
    deepseek_v4_fp8_einsum_config (SM100 packed only on cap.major==10, else SM90-shaped
    (1,128,128) FP32 block scales).
  - Dispatcher extended to accept 3D b (is_bmm B12X weight layout), not just 2D.
  - The einsum is invoked through torch.ops.vllm.deepseek_v4_fp8_o_proj_einsum custom op
    (direct_register_custom_op) so dynamo keeps it opaque — without this boundary dynamo tries
    to trace get_device_capability() and fails.
- Docs: docs/contributing/kernel_targets/sm120.md documents the DeepGEMM fp8_einsum SM12x
  incompatibility and Triton fallback dispatch.
- Checks: py_compile for fp8_einsum.py and o_proj.py passes; git diff --check passes.

2026-08-17 fp8_einsum Triton predicate dtype fix:
- After the dynamo boundary fix, the Triton path still didn't fire: the dispatcher fell through
  to DeepGEMM (layout.hpp:107) because the B12X weight path stores raw E8M0 scale bits as
  torch.uint8 (weight_scale_inv.format_ue8m0=True), but _use_deepseek_v4_sm12x_triton_fp8_einsum
  only accepted float32 / float8_e8m0fnu, and the kernel upcast only handled float8_e8m0fnu.
- Fix: predicate now accepts torch.uint8 (plus float32/e8m0); both kernel wrappers
  (einsum + quant) upcast torch.uint8 / float8_e8m0fnu scales to fp32 before launch.
  _upcast_e8m0_to_fp32 already handles uint8 (view->int32<<23).
- Checks: py_compile + git diff --check pass.

2026-08-17 B12X CUTLASS DSL cute-to-nvvm ICE root cause:
- Symptom (recurring): b12x w4a16 MoE / WO-projection JIT compile fails with
  "ValueError: failed to add `cute-to-nvvm` with options ... cubin-chip=sm_121a (or sm_120f)"
  wrapped as a CUTLASS DSLRuntimeError ICE. Independent of arch string (sm_121a and sm_120f
  both fail), DSL version (4.6.2 and 4.7.0 both fail), and b12x fork pin (fork pin, fork master,
  and local-inference-lab are identical on the failing w4a16 kernel).
- Reproduced standalone in the aarch64 image:
    CompileOptions().to_str() -> "opt-level=3 enable-pyir=false ..."
    PassManager.parse("...enable-pyir=false...")   -> ValueError: no such option enable-pyir
    PassManager.parse("...(without enable-pyir)...") -> OK
  So the Python frontend (nvidia_cutlass_dsl.compiler) unconditionally serializes
  `enable-pyir=false` into the cute-to-nvvm pipeline, but the native MLIR pass in the aarch64
  wheel does not register that option, so PassManager.parse rejects the whole pipeline before
  compiling any kernel. Root cause is Python/native version skew baked into the aarch64 wheel.
- Fix (build-time, in install-runtime-wheels.sh): patch compiler.py so BooleanCompileOption
  only emits the option when its value is truthy (enable-pyir defaults false and B12X never
  uses the PyIR/FrontendNext preprocessor, so dropping the token is a no-op semantically).
  Verified patched serialize() drops enable-pyir=false and PassManager.parse succeeds.

2026-08-17 B12X cu13 runtime missing TVM FFI symbols:
- After the enable-pyir patch, the DSL compiled but JIT link failed with
  "Symbols not found: [ CuteDSLRT_TVMFFISetRaisedCudaError, CuteDSLRT_TVMFFISetRaisedCudaLaunchError ]".
- Root cause: the cu13 runtime lib (libcute_dsl_runtime_cu13.so) is NVIDIA's cross-compiled
  (SBSA) build and does NOT export the CuteDSLRT_TVMFFI* symbols, while the cu12 runtime lib
  does. The generated host stub references them unconditionally, so linking against cu13 fails.
  NVIDIA docs confirm cross-compiled DSL libs are not TVM-FFI compatible; the plain AOT export
  path is expected. (apache-tvm-ffi was already in requirements/cuda.txt; the missing symbol is
  in the native lib, not a missing pip package.)
- Workaround (runtime env var, no rebuild): set CUTE_DSL_LIBS to the cu12 runtime lib so the
  JIT links a runtime that exports the TVM FFI symbols.

2026-08-17 B12X w4a16 route-packing expert_counts CUDA-graph-capture fix:
- After the DSL compiled and linked, the model got past torch.compile and B12X MoE warmup but
  crashed at CUDA graph capture with
  "expert_counts is not initialized for CUDA graph capture; provide a preallocated W4A16
  route-packing workspace" from route_pack._workspace_slice.
- Root cause: b12x fused_moe/_impl.py only added expert_counts to the core workspace plan and
  only passed it to the launch when full_rotation=True, but pack_topk_routes_by_expert requires
  it on the non-full-rotation dynamic path too (unconditionally slices it). So during capture
  it was None and route_pack tried to allocate inside the graph.
- Fix (fork source + baked into wheel): always include the (route_E,) int32 expert_counts spec
  in _plan_core_workspace tensor_specs and always pass binding.expert_counts at launch
  (drop the `if full_rotation else None`). Arena sizing/mapping are spec-driven
  (_core_workspace_nbytes / _map_core_workspace_views iterate tensor_specs), so no other change
  needed; route_num_experts == prepared.num_experts == route_E matches the tensor shape.
- Checks: py_compile of fused_moe/_impl.py and w4a16/kernel.py passes.

